    "title": "Concise plain and poetic visual copy",
    "uri": "instavar://creative-guides/visual-copy",
    "text": "# Write concise text for visual stories\n\nStatus: practical guidance, adapted from documented writing methods and bounded human feedback. This is not a measured guarantee of comprehension or engagement.\n\nUse for carousel headlines, supporting text, captions and video scripts. Begin with plain language. Add poetic expression when the brief asks for an experience or mood; keep instructions, factual claims and interface states literal.\n\n## Start with the viewer and the evidence\n\nName the audience, what they already know, the one idea they should understand, and the action or feeling the piece invites. List supplied facts and necessary qualifications separately from imagined details. Inspect the accompanying image before describing it. If it is unavailable, ask for context or mark the visual description as a proposal.\n\nDecide what the image demonstrates and what the words must add. A caption can rely on an accompanying picture only when the intended viewer receives that picture. Provide a self-contained alternative description for accessibility and text-only reuse.\n\n## Expand, select, connect, compress\n\n1. Expand enough to understand the full thought. Find the concrete objects, actions, relationships, evidence and limits.\n2. Select what must survive. In an explanation, preserve the causal link, units, conditions and uncertainty. In poetic copy, preserve the scene, voice and details that carry the experience.\n3. Connect the ideas in the order a new viewer needs them. Each slide or scene should advance the explanation or experience.\n4. Compress by removing repeated meaning, vague labels and redundant explanations. Read it again against the source. Shorter wording must not make the claim stronger.\n\nTreat brevity as revision pressure, not a word-count target. If a condition matters to the decision, retain it close to that decision. A tiny footnote or a later slide may be missed when the current slide is shared alone.\n\n## Use poetic language selectively\n\nLet a concrete action or sensory detail suggest emotion before naming the emotion. Choose details that belong to this scene. Movement, posture, pressure, temperature, hesitation and small corrections can matter as much as sight or sound. Do not force every sense into a passage.\n\nMetaphor, personification and rhythm should serve one governing idea. Remove mixed metaphors and phrases that could describe any product. Leave room for interpretation in expressive copy, but do not leave factual instructions ambiguous. Do not invent an observed event, testimonial, feeling or product result. Sensuality is an optional requested mode, not a default meaning of poetic.\n\n## Draft examples\n\nThese are newly authored teaching examples, not approved campaign copy.\n\n| Purpose             | Weak draft                                    | More concrete draft                                                   | What must remain                                        |\n| ------------------- | --------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Product instruction | Experience seamless creative transformation.  | Choose a clip. Add your caption. Preview the result.                  | Name only actions the product actually supports.        |\n| Quiet atmosphere    | Discover the magic of a new morning.          | The kettle clicks. Light reaches the table.                           | Use only supplied or explicitly imagined scene details. |\n| Evidence summary    | This format makes everyone understand faster. | In this review, two readers found the revised chart easier to follow. | Counts, population and evidence source must be real.    |\n\n## Deliver copy the creator can use\n\nReturn the title and caption separately. For a carousel or video, give one row per slide or scene: purpose, on-screen text, visual evidence, optional narration, and necessary qualification. Keep narration separate from on-screen copy so the screen need not repeat every spoken sentence.\n\nUse line breaks to shape meaningful groups. A single newline can keep short actions together; a blank line can mark a change of scene or thought. Follow the requested channel formatting. Neither spacing rule is universal.\n\nReview the actual render at the intended viewing size. Read the headline sequence alone, then the complete piece. Ask an unfamiliar viewer what happened, why, and what they would do next. A correct schema or short sentence does not establish reader understanding.\n\n## Reusable request\n\n“Using the supplied facts and visuals, draft concise copy for [audience] in [carousel/video format]. The viewer should understand [idea]. Use [plain/poetic] language. Give each scene a clear job, keep necessary conditions and evidence, and separate on-screen text from narration. Mark missing facts rather than inventing them. Return title, scene copy, caption and a short source-preservation check.”\n\nSee the companion carousel, design and human-review guides. Source provenance and scope are recorded in the repository's September 2026 creative-playbook transfer report.\n"
