    "id": "carousel-stories",
    "title": "Carousel story structures and transfer limits",
    "uri": "instavar://creative-guides/carousel-stories",
    "text": "# Choose a carousel story structure\n\nStatus: planning recipes adapted from the Eclat carousel programme. Recipes are not newly ported renderers or proof of audience performance.\n\nChoose a structure for the relationship being explained. A topic is not a template: circular motion, collisions and half-life can share a questioning structure while needing different diagrams, conditions and notation.\n\n## Existing Instavar templates\n\nThe HTTP template MCP currently scaffolds `listicle`, `manifesto`, `before-after` and `value-first-reveal`. Use `list_carousel_templates` and `get_carousel_schema` for the executable contract. The npm connector's video tools are a separate catalogue; these recipes do not imply npm carousel rendering support.\n\n- Listicle: parallel points that belong together.\n- Manifesto: one idea per slide in a connected progression.\n- Before/after: a genuine two-state contrast. This builder uses the first and last point; do not put essential intermediate reasoning into points that will be omitted.\n- Value-first reveal: useful information followed by evidence and a restrained product reveal. Do not invent product proof to fill a slot.\n\n## Newer educational recipes\n\n| Recipe                                 | Reading path                                                                                                                    | Use when                                                        | Preserve                                                         |\n| -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Question, evidence, explanation, check | Ask a specific question; show the relevant objects; explain the relationship; compare a case; ask a final check with its answer | A plausible misconception can be resolved visually              | The conditions that make the explanation true; a complete answer |\n| Observation to conclusion              | Show an observation; separate it from inference; compare explanations; state what can be concluded                              | The viewer must reason from visible evidence                    | Uncertainty and alternative explanations                         |\n| Worked comparison                      | Establish objects and quantities; show the symbolic relationship; substitute values; compare outcomes; conclude                 | A calculation needs to stay connected to the physical situation | Units, signs, definitions and unchanged variables                |\n| Action and correction                  | Show the setup; show an action; show a small correction; show the result                                                        | A practical action or sensory moment carries the story          | Safe technique, sequence and a truthful visible result           |\n\nThese can be drafted with one idea per slide using the manifesto skeleton, then reviewed and edited. Diagrams, equation blocks, editable placeholders and scientific apparatus require an appropriate construction layer; the text scaffold does not create or verify them. Never flatten a necessary diagram into decorative background artwork.\n\n## Lessons from the newer drafts\n\nKeep the relevant objects beside their equations so the viewer can connect symbols to the situation. State a useful symbolic relationship before numerical substitution when that helps the intended reader. Typeset fractions and symbols rather than using ambiguous slash text.\n\nGroup related words with their diagram. Give each calculation slide one main job. Remove duplicate results when the chart already carries them, but retain necessary assumptions. Make supporting text readable at delivery size. Recheck the whole deck after a local change.\n\nThe September 2026 Eclat batch included photoelectric threshold, circular motion, terminal velocity, sticking collision, wave amplitude, SHM turning points, standing waves, single-slit diffraction, field versus potential and half-life. A later six-deck revision applied earlier feedback to circular motion, SHM, standing waves, diffraction, field/potential and half-life. Those revised drafts were awaiting human review in the inspected record. This topic inventory is provenance, not a claim of ten new Instavar templates.\n\n## Delivery check\n\nReview full masters and every intended crop. A centred 4:5 crop of a 9:16 master was an Eclat production choice, not a universal platform requirement. Keep essential text, diagrams and qualifications inside the actual delivery region; verify current platform constraints separately before publication.\n\nTransfer the story method. Choose branding, type, texture, colour and imagery for the current creator. Eclat logos, source images, specialist answers and approval labels do not transfer automatically.\n"
