    "id": "visual-design",
    "title": "UI, graphic design and video principles",
    "uri": "instavar://creative-guides/visual-design",
    "text": "# Make meaning visible across slides, video and interfaces\n\nStatus: practical design and review guidance. Context-specific aesthetic and outcome claims still need audience evidence.\n\n## Give attention a useful path\n\nStart with the audience's task. Identify what should be noticed first, what it means, where attention moves next, and what evidence supports the conclusion. Use hierarchy to express that path. The first meaningful text should usually carry the main idea; a small label above it earns its place only when it changes interpretation.\n\nMatch form to relationship: sequence for steps, side-by-side comparison for alternatives, a diagram for a mechanism, and an explicit state change for an action. Do not force every relationship into the same card layout. Make the heading sequence tell a connected story while the full reading supplies proof and limits.\n\n## Graphic design\n\nUse proximity, alignment, spacing, scale, weight and contrast to show grouping and priority. Keep type and colour roles consistent. Test text against its actual background and at its final size; do not assume a colour token alone guarantees contrast. Keep non-colour cues for important distinctions.\n\nTypography and colour can suggest tone, but their meanings depend on audience and context. A serif does not prove quality and a particular hue does not guarantee trust. Choose an expressive treatment only when the core content remains legible.\n\nReview local details, the whole image and the collection separately. A sharper object or stronger accent may weaken overall harmony. Compare against the verified source image; a file labelled original can still be an edited derivative. Retaining the original is a valid outcome.\n\n## Video and motion\n\nPlan what changes in the viewer's understanding across time. Show the setup before an action that requires it. Keep spatial and causal continuity when the viewer must follow a procedure. Use cuts, movement and sound to reveal a relationship or shift attention, not simply to add activity.\n\nGive viewers enough time to read the actual text and inspect the evidence. Test at normal playback speed. Treat muted review, picture review, narration review and the combined sound mix as separate checks. Time visible contact and its sound together when synchrony matters. Keep necessary information available when audio is absent.\n\nDo not import film pacing into interactive controls blindly. A film controls its timeline; an interface must preserve user agency, orientation and recovery.\n\n## UI and editing workflows\n\nMake the current object, possible action, saved state and consequence clear. Keep frequent controls visible at the intended viewport. Distinguish selection, dragging and text editing. Provide undo and understandable conflict handling for editable work. A preview and its export should reflect the same saved document.\n\nThese are review questions, not claims that the Eclat prototype's editor has been integrated into Instavar. Prototype gestures, exact button layouts and crop choices require fresh implementation and testing in the receiving product.\n\n## Verify the artifact\n\nInspect final-size text, crops, diagram labels, symbols, contrast, grouping and the overall sequence. For interfaces, also exercise keyboard use, focus, narrow viewports, saving, errors and recovery. For video, review timing and sound in playback rather than relying only on contact sheets.\n\nAutomated checks establish their measured properties, such as dimensions, parsing or bounds. They do not establish human preference, comprehension or campaign performance. Ask a new viewer to explain the idea or perform the intended task before making those stronger claims.\n"
