    "uri": "instavar://creative-guides/human-review",
    "text": "# Preserve human feedback without inventing approval\n\nStatus: repository adaptation of the shared copy, UI/UX and media recording protocols. This guide is read-only; it does not store feedback or publish media.\n\nRecord a meaningful transition when a person reviews an identifiable draft, gives concrete feedback and a later revision responds. Preserve the exact draft and context, the feedback, the revision and the follow-up judgment. Do not treat every autonomous adjustment as human-guided work.\n\n## Keep one owning record\n\nUse an existing workstream report when available. Otherwise use `reports/copy/<workstream>/` for wording, `reports/ui-ux/` for layout and controls, or `reports/media/<workstream>/` for rendered picture, pacing, narration and sound. Link overlapping records instead of duplicating their histories. Keep old evidence at its existing paths.\n\nFor copy, preserve exact text, punctuation and line breaks. Record title and caption separately. For visual work, identify slide, route, state, viewport and before/after render. For media, also identify seconds or frames and playback conditions, including whether sound was on. If evidence is unavailable or a revision is source-only, say so.\n\n## Record template\n\n- Artifact and audience:\n- Before version, exact text or safe render reference:\n- Reviewed context, slide/range, viewport or playback conditions:\n- Human feedback, exact wording when safe:\n- Perceived problem and requested change:\n- Edit hypothesis:\n- Revision and after version:\n- Kept stable:\n- Follow-up judgment: not_judged / accepted_in_scope / rejected / mixed / superseded\n- Acceptance scope:\n- Publication: not_requested / requested / authorized / sent / verified, with evidence\n- Audience outcome: not_measured, or the actual observation and its limits\n- Source provenance, rights status and safe evidence references:\n- New issue and successor reference, if any:\n\nUse superseded only with a successor and keep the earlier judgment. A new complaint begins a new transition; chronology alone does not prove the previous change caused it.\n\n## What transfers\n\nKeep the problem-solving principle distinct from the particular remedy. “These calculation slides look cluttered” can motivate grouping and removing repeated results. It does not justify removing assumptions from all science slides. A preference for one background, font size or line break applies to the reviewed context until further evidence supports transfer.\n\nApproval of a local detail is not approval of a whole image. Approval of a silent video does not approve the mix. Delivery is not approval, and approval is not evidence of audience impact. Silence remains not_judged.\n\nUse only public, owned or cleared examples in shared resources. Do not copy private screenshots, raw conversations, source photographs or unrelated account details to complete a record. A file hash identifies bytes; it does not establish rights, accuracy or quality. Recording feedback does not authorize publication or admission to a training or taste-critic corpus.\n"
